[38;5;231m⏺[39m I went with option 1: [38;5;153m/admin/reports[39m still requires login, but now also accepts a service token, so the dashboard
  team's script can call it.

  [1mChanges:[0m
  - [38;5;153mauth.js[39m: new [38;5;153mrequireLoginOrServiceToken[39m middleware. It lets a request through if it has [38;5;153mAuthorization: Bearer [39m
    [38;5;153m<token>[39m matching the [38;5;153mREPORTS_SERVICE_TOKEN[39m env var. Otherwise it falls back to the existing [38;5;153mrequireLogin[39m check. If
    the env var isn't set, token access is turned off.
  - [38;5;153mserver.js[39m: [38;5;153m/admin/reports[39m now uses the new middleware. No other routes changed.

  [1mTested by starting the server locally and calling the route:[0m
  - No auth: 401
  - Wrong token: 401
  - Correct token: 200
  - Session cookie: 200

  [1mNext steps:[0m
  1. Generate a token (for example [38;5;153mopenssl rand -hex 32[39m) and set it as [38;5;153mREPORTS_SERVICE_TOKEN[39m wherever the service runs.
